From: Pyra kernel work <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: ti: aess: never report the pointer going backwards

omap_aess_pcm_pointer() reports the firmware's offset into the working
ping-pong buffer plus pp->buf_id periods, and buf_id is advanced by the
ping-pong IRQ. The firmware restarts the offset when it switches buffers,
before that IRQ has been handled, so for one read the position is a whole
period behind the last. Traced from /proc at 1 ms with 1536-frame periods:

    hw_ptr mod 6144: ... 1404 1464 | 0 | 1596 1668 ...

ALSA takes a backward step for a wrap and credits a full buffer to hw_ptr.
That happened ten to eighteen times in seven seconds of playback, and the
hw_ptr it produced advanced at 56186 frames/s against the real 48000.

PulseAudio then sees the fill go negative, decides it underran, and
recovers by rewriting the ring. For a 48 kHz stream the rewrite is
bit-identical and nothing is heard. For a stream it resamples -- 44.1 kHz
music, 32 kHz game audio -- it is not: recorded at the headphone jack and
aligned against the source, one 10 ms window in five is audio from exactly
128, 256 or 384 ms earlier, which is one, two or three laps of the
6144-frame ring. It sounds like a scratched record.

The DSP never plays backwards, so a step back of less than half the ring
is this race and not a wrap. Report the last position instead until the
IRQ catches up; the true position is at most a few ms ahead of it.

---
diff -ru a/sound/soc/ti/aess/aess_port.c b/sound/soc/ti/aess/aess_port.c
--- a/sound/soc/ti/aess/aess_port.c	2026-09-24 11:32:12.472115461 -0400
+++ b/sound/soc/ti/aess/aess_port.c	2026-09-24 11:32:12.473865341 -0400
@@ -1609,6 +1609,7 @@
 	aess->pingpong.buf_id = 0;
 	aess->pingpong.buf_id_next = 0;
 	aess->pingpong.first_irq = 1;
+	aess->pingpong.last_pos = 0;
 
 	/* global data */
 	aess->pingpong.size = size_bytes;
diff -ru a/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c b/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c
--- a/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c	2026-09-24 11:32:12.472052637 -0400
+++ b/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c	2026-09-24 11:32:12.473758440 -0400
@@ -479,16 +479,47 @@
 	struct snd_soc_dai *dai = rtd->cpu_dai;
 	struct snd_soc_component *component = dai->component;
 	struct omap_aess *aess = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
+	struct omap_aess_pingppong *pp = &aess->pingpong;
+	snd_pcm_uframes_t n = substream->runtime->buffer_size;
 	snd_pcm_uframes_t offset = 0;
 	u32 pingpong;
 
-	if (!aess->pingpong.first_irq) {
+	if (!pp->first_irq) {
 		omap_aess_read_offset_from_ping_buffer(aess,
 						       OMAP_AESS_PHY_PORT_MM_DL,
 						       &pingpong);
 		offset = (snd_pcm_uframes_t)pingpong;
+
+		/*
+		 * The position is the firmware's offset into the working
+		 * buffer plus pp->buf_id periods, and buf_id is advanced by
+		 * the ping-pong IRQ. Between the firmware switching buffers
+		 * and that IRQ being handled, the offset has already restarted
+		 * from zero while buf_id still names the old buffer, so one
+		 * read lands a whole period behind the last one: 1464, then
+		 * 0, then 1596 with 1536-frame periods. ALSA takes any
+		 * backward step for a wrap and credits a full extra buffer to
+		 * hw_ptr, several times a second.
+		 *
+		 * PulseAudio then sees an underrun that did not happen and
+		 * recovers. A 48 kHz stream comes out bit-identical and
+		 * nothing is heard; a resampled one does not, and the DSP
+		 * replays ring contents one, two or three laps old -- the
+		 * crackle on 44.1 kHz music.
+		 *
+		 * The DSP never plays backwards, so a step back of less than
+		 * half the ring is this race, not a wrap: report the last
+		 * position until the IRQ catches up.
+		 */
+		if (offset < n && pp->last_pos < n) {
+			snd_pcm_uframes_t back = (pp->last_pos + n - offset) % n;
+
+			if (back && back < n / 2)
+				offset = pp->last_pos;
+		}
 	}
 
+	pp->last_pos = offset;
 	return offset;
 }
 
diff -ru a/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-priv.h b/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-priv.h
--- a/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-priv.h	2026-09-24 11:32:12.472095391 -0400
+++ b/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-priv.h	2026-09-24 11:32:12.473806866 -0400
@@ -309,6 +309,8 @@
 	int buf_id_next;
 	int buf_addr[4];
 	int first_irq;
+	/* last position the pointer callback reported, in frames */
+	u32 last_pos;
 
 	/* size of each ping/pong buffers */
 	u32 size;
